  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n6+9+12+\u2026=195\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=195,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=6.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =9-6 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 195 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(6)+(N-1)(3)) \\\\ 390 & =N(12+3N-3) \\\\ 390 & =N(9+3N) \\\\ 390 & =3{N}^{2}+9N \\\\ 3{N}^{2}+9N-390 & =0 \\\\ {N}^{2}+3N-130 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-130)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{529}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 23}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=10$ or $N=-13.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms.",
